{
  "title": "Enchondromas",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pathology/8018/enchondromas",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Apr 7 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Enchondromas are benign latent cartilaginous tumors composed of hyaline cartilage that typically occur in medullary cavity of the diaphysis or metaphysis. Patients typically present between the ages of 20-50 with an asymptomatic lesion, discovered incidentally on radiographs.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made radiographically with the presence of a well-defined, lucent, central medullary lesion that is 1-10 cm, and often associated with \"pop-corn\" stippling, arcs, whorls, or rings.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is observation as most lesions are asymptomatic.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "2nd most common benign cartilage lesion (osteochondroma is most common)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "male:female ratio is **1:1**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "age bracket",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "most common in **20-50 year olds**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "usually found in the medullary cavity of the **diaphysis** or **metaphysis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "the most common locations **hand** (60%) > **feet**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "the **most common primary bone tumor** in the hand is the enchondroma",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "other locations include **distal femur** (20%) > **proximal humerus** (10%) > tibia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "invole IDH-1 & IDH-2 gene",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "enchondromas represent incomplete endochondral ossification",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "chondroblasts and fragments of epiphyseal cartilage **escape from the physis, displace into the metaphysis and proliferate there**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "malignant transformation in <1% to chondrosarcoma",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Ollier's disease (multiple enchondromatosis)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/ollier%20foot.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/olliers%20hand.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "sporadic inheritance with no genetic predisposition",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "skeletal dysplasia with failure of normal endochondral ossification",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "enchondromas throughout the **metaphyses** and **diaphyses** of long bones",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "involved bones are dysplastic, with shortening and bowing",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "risk of malignant transformation <30%",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**Maffucci's syndrome**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "sporadic inheritance with no genetic predisposition",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**distinguished by soft-tissue angiomas**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/maffucci%20hand.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    },
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/maffucci%20foot.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Marked expansion of lesion on radiographs, with angiomas are seen as small, round **calcified phleboliths**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "increased risk of **visceral malignancies** (astrocytoma, GI malignancy)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "overall risk of developing any malignancy up to **100%**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**asymptomatic,** discovered **incidentally on radiographs**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "usually true for enchondromas in long bones and foot",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**pathologic fracture**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "often seen with enchondromas in the hand",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/patho%20fracture.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    },
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/patho%20fracture%202.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**pain**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "pain is uncommon",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "more likely in hand and feet secondary to pathologic fractures",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "differentiates from **chondrosarcomas (non-mechanical pain rest pain and nocturnal pain)**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "shortening and angular deformities (in inherited diseases)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/maffucci%20hand2.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/enchondroma%20-%20clinical%20phot%20-%20knee%20angulation_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/enchondroma%20-%20xray%20-%20knee%20angulation_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "enchondromas may disrupt the growth plate",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "multiple bluish angiomas in Maffucci's syndrome",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "usually diagnostic",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "well defined, lucent, central medullary lesions that calcify over time",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "1 to 10cm in size",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**metaphyseal** location when they first appear",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "appear more **diaphyseal** as the long bone grows",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**\"pop-corn\" stippling**, **arcs, whorls, rings**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/Case%20B%20-%20prox%20humerus%20-%20xray%20-%20Parsons.png",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "minimal endosteal erosion (<50% width of cortex)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "cortical expansion and thinning may be present in hands, feet (inherited diseases)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/maffucci%20hand%20xr1.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    },
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/maffucci%20hand%20xr2.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "may have purely lytic appearance",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/Case%20E%20-%20distal%20phalanx%20-%20xray%20-%20Parsons_moved.png",
                          "caption": "lytic appearance in the digit"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "skeletal survey if polycystic disease is suspected",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Ollier's disease",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "bones are dysplastic, with shortening and bowing",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "Maffucci's syndrome",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "angiomas are visible as **calcified phleboliths**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**unlike chondrosarcomas**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "cortical thickening and destruction",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "endosteal erosions and scalloping >50% of the width of the cortex",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "are larger (>5cm)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Bone scan",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "indications",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "help differentiate from chondrosarcoma",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "to identify **polyostotic** disease",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "rarely adds useful information",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "only 20% have more uptake than ASIS",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "most enchondromas are small, and easily identified as benign",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/bone%20scan%20.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/bone%20scan%202.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "increased uptake due to continued remodeling within lesion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "less uptake than chondrosarcoma",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "MRI",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "lobule and bright on T2 without marrow edema or periosteal reaction",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "indications",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "usually not necessary for diagnosis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "identify size and intramedullary extent and soft tissue extension",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "differentiate from chondrosarcoma and bony infarcts",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/t1%20distal%20femur.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/t2%20distal%20femur.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "lobular and bright on T2-weighted images",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/t2%20distal%20femur.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "no bone marrow edema or periosteal reaction",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "may show steak of cartilage or \"sled runner tracks\"",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/Case%20C%20-%20sled%20runners%20MRI_moved.png",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "medullary fill >90% suggests chondrosarcoma instead",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Studies",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Core needle-biopsy",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "from areas of **bone scalloping** or lysis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "prone to sampling error due to tumor heterogeneity",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "chondrosarcomas may contain areas of benign hyaline cartilage",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "often impossible to differentiate from low-grade chondrosarcoma",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Histology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "gross appearance",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "blue gray, lobulated hyaline cartilage, with scattered calcifications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "microscopic",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "mature hyaline cartilage lobules separated by normal marrow",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "solitary lesions in long bones",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "hypocellular with **bland, mature hyaline cartilage (blue balls of cartilage) separated by normal marrow**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "differentiates from chondrosarcoma",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "endochondral ossification encases cartilage with **lamellar bone**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**abundant extracellular matrix with no myxoid component**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "solitary lesions in small tubular bones and fibula",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**hypercellular, with mild chondrocytic atypia**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "characteristics of chondrocytes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**small, bland chondroid cells in lacunar spaces**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "uniform staining nuclei",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "no pleomorphism, mitoses, anaplasia, hyperchromasia or multinucleate cells",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "3",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "unlike **chondrosarcomas**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "hypercellularity, with plump nuclei",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "multiple binucleate cells",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "giant cells with clumps of chromatin",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Differential",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Bone infarct**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "\"smoke up the chimney\" radiographic appearance",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8018/images/bone%20infarct%20xray_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "MRI has low T2 signal",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Chondrosarcoma**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "deep endosteal scalloping >2/3 of cortical thickness",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "more common in pelvis, scapula, ribs",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "observation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "treatment for vast majority of asymptomatic enchondromas",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "follow up",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**serial radiographs** at 6 months and 12 months to confirm radiographic stability",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "**long term follow-up** for patients with multiple enchondroma syndromes",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "intralesional curettage and bone grafting",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "lesion that shows any **change on serial xrays**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "symptomatic lesions",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "pathologic fractures",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "immediate curettage and grafting are now favored",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "-1",
                          "children": []
                        },
                        {
                          "text": "no difference in outcomes between delayed and immediate curettage and grafting",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "4",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "radiographs suspicious for low-grade chondrosarcoma",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "large lesions at risk for recurrent fracture",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "outcomes",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "local recurrence is unusual",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Complications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Malignant transformation**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "risk of transformation of enchondroma to chondrosarcoma",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "solitary enchondroma",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "risk of malignant transformation is **1%**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "Ollier's disease",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "risk of malignant transformation is **25-30%**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "Maffucci's syndrome",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "risk of malignant transformation is **25-30%**, but up to **100%** risk of other visceral and CNS malignancies as well",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
